from engine import Author
A=Author('22');add=A.add
add('5.2.1-01','Electric current is the rate of flow of charge.','It is measured in amperes using an ammeter; 1 A = 1 C/s.','A current is not the total amount of charge stored on an object. The same charge crossing a section over a shorter interval corresponds to greater average current. In a metal, electrons provide the mobile charge carriers; the conventional current direction is defined using positive-charge motion.','State the SI unit of electric current.','Ampere, A.')
add('5.2.1-02','An ammeter measures the current passing through it.','It is connected in series with the branch whose current is measured.','Open the low-voltage circuit and insert the meter so branch charge passes through it. Choose an appropriate range and polarity for DC measurement. An ideal ammeter has negligible resistance; connecting it directly across a source can create a damaging large-current path. Use a current-limited classroom supply and switch off before changing connections.','Describe the connection of an ammeter to measure a lamp’s current.','Connect it in series with the lamp in that branch, using a suitable range and polarity.')
add('5.2.1-03','Average current is I = Q/t.','Charge is Q = It when a constant current flows for time t.','A 0.30 A current for 20 s transfers 6 C across a section. Use seconds rather than unconverted minutes and amperes rather than unconverted milliamperes for the SI calculation. A current need not consume charge as it passes through a resistor; the energy transfer and charge flow are different descriptions.','Calculate the current when 12 C passes a point in 30 s.','I = Q/t = 12/30.~The average current is 0.40 A.')
add('5.2.1-04','Electrostatics concerns charge distributions approximately at rest.','Current electricity concerns charge flow through a path.','A rubbed insulating rod can retain a net charge locally, while a complete conducting circuit can support sustained current from a source. An electrostatic discharge is itself a brief current, so the two descriptions are connected rather than unrelated forms of substance. Charge is conserved in both.','Explain the distinction between static and current electricity.','Static electricity concerns an approximately stationary charge distribution.~Current electricity concerns charge flowing through a path.')
add('5.2.1-05','AC reverses direction periodically, while DC flows in one direction.','In a metal, electron drift is opposite to conventional current.','Two different comparisons are involved. AC versus DC describes whether the current’s direction changes with time. Electron versus conventional direction describes the carrier’s sign relative to a defined positive-current direction. A varying one-direction current is still DC, so constant magnitude is not the defining requirement.','Explain why AC/DC and electron/conventional direction describe different features of current.','Link AC/DC to whether current reverses with time.~Electron drift versus conventional direction concerns the negative carrier moving opposite to the positive-charge convention.')
add('5.2.1-05-M01','Alternating current reverses direction periodically.','Direct current keeps one direction, although its magnitude can vary.','On a signed current-time graph, a typical AC trace crosses zero into opposite signs. A pulsed trace that stays on one side is one-direction current despite changing size. A cell is a common classroom DC source. Do not assume that any changing current must be alternating.','Explain the difference between alternating and direct current.','AC reverses direction periodically.~DC keeps one direction, even if its magnitude changes.')
add('5.2.1-05-M02','Conventional current follows the defined direction of positive-charge flow.','In a metal circuit, negative electrons drift in the opposite direction.','In the external circuit of a discharging cell, conventional current goes from positive toward negative through the load; electrons drift from negative toward positive. The microscopic random electron motion is superimposed on a much smaller drift. This drift direction is distinct from the speed at which circuit changes propagate.','Explain how electron-flow direction compares with conventional current in a metal.','Electron drift is opposite to conventional current.~This is because electrons are negative while conventional direction is defined for positive charge.')
add('5.2.2-01','Electromotive force is energy supplied by a source per unit charge.','Despite its name, e.m.f. is not a force measured in newtons.','A 1.5 V cell supplies about 1.5 J per coulomb in the specified model. It converts energy from a store such as chemical energy into the circuit’s electrical transfer pathway. For identical series-aiding cells under open-circuit conditions, their voltages add because each contributes energy per charge; a high-resistance voltmeter measures this approximately without substantial load current.','Define electromotive force.','It is the energy supplied by a source per unit charge.')
add('5.2.2-02','Source e.m.f. is ε = E/Q for the source’s supplied energy.','One volt is one joule per coulomb.','A source supplying 18 J to 6 C has e.m.f. 3 V. The denominator is charge, not time. If the source has internal resistance and a load draws current, terminal potential difference can differ from e.m.f.; the basic energy-per-charge definition remains. No internal-resistance calculation is required for this task.','Calculate source e.m.f. for 24 J supplied per 8 C.','ε = E/Q = 24/8.~The e.m.f. is 3 V.')
add('5.2.2-03','Potential difference is energy transferred per charge between two points.','Across a component, V = W/Q.','If 15 J transfers as 5 C passes a lamp, the potential difference is 3 V. The charge need not disappear after giving energy to the lamp. An analogy can compare the same amount of circulating charge at different energy levels, but it must not imply charge is fuel consumed by the component.','Calculate potential difference when 20 J transfers as 4 C passes through a component.','V = W/Q = 20/4.~The potential difference is 5 V.')
add('5.2.2-04','Both e.m.f. and potential difference use volts.','They describe energy-per-charge changes with different roles.','The same unit does not make a source and a load identical. A source supplies energy per charge; a component can transfer it to light, internal energy or mechanical output. Label which pair of points or which source is being considered.','State the unit used for both e.m.f. and potential difference.','Volt, V.')
add('5.2.2-05','A voltmeter measures potential difference between its two terminals.','It is connected in parallel across the chosen component or pair of points.','An ideal voltmeter has very high resistance so it draws negligible current and disturbs the circuit little. Use a suitable DC range and correct polarity in a cell circuit. Putting it in series would substantially alter the current rather than simply measuring the intended component voltage.','Describe how to connect a voltmeter to measure potential difference across a lamp.','Connect it in parallel across the lamp’s two terminals, using a suitable range and polarity.')
add('5.2.2-06','A cell’s terminal potential difference can drive current through a complete conducting path.','A broken circuit prevents steady ordinary current around that path.','The voltage can exist across open terminals even when current is negligible. Closing a suitable path allows a field to establish charge drift through its components. A source voltage alone therefore does not prove current is flowing: the path and its properties also matter.','State the additional circuit condition needed for a cell to drive steady current through a lamp.','A complete conducting path through the cell and lamp is needed.')
add('5.2.2-07','E.m.f. describes source energy supplied per charge, while a load’s potential difference describes energy transferred per charge there.','Both quantities use V or J/C.','A complete circuit’s energy accounting compares source input with transfers across its components. In a simple ideal source and one-load model, their numerical values can match, but their physical roles remain distinct. With several series components, their potential differences share the source contribution.','Explain how source e.m.f. differs from a lamp’s potential difference.','E.m.f. is energy supplied by the source per unit charge.~The lamp’s potential difference is energy transferred at the lamp per unit charge.')
A.misconception('MIS-19','Energy per charge is potential difference, while energy per time is power.','The different denominators give different physical quantities and units.','A component transferring 18 J while 6 C passes has 3 V across it. If the event takes 2 s, its average power is 9 W. Both descriptions can apply to the same event, but one cannot replace charge with time merely because both values appear in a question.','A lamp transfers 18 J as 6 C passes in 2 s. Explain why dividing 18 by 2 does not give its potential difference.','The error divides energy by time, giving power of 9 W.~The correct potential difference divides by charge: 18/6 = 3 V.')
C=A.choice
C('5.2.1-01','What does current measure?',('Charge flow per time','This gives C/s or A.'),('Energy per charge','This is voltage.'),('Energy per time','This is power.'),('Stored mass','This is not current.'))
C('5.2.1-02','Where is an ammeter placed to measure branch current?',('In series in that branch','The measured charge flows through it.'),('Directly across a source','That can create a short circuit.'),('Parallel with no regard to the branch','It must measure the intended path.'),('Outside the circuit with no connection','It cannot measure conduction current there.'))
C('5.2.2-05','How is a voltmeter connected across a resistor?',('In parallel','Its terminals compare the resistor’s endpoints.'),('In series only','That would alter the circuit strongly.'),('As a zero-resistance short','It should have high resistance.'),('To one terminal only','A two-point difference needs both connections.'))
C('5.2.1-05-M01','Which feature defines AC?',('Periodic direction reversal','Magnitude variation alone is insufficient.'),('A fixed positive direction','That is DC.'),('Only electron motion with no field','This is not the defining feature.'),('Zero voltage everywhere','That does not describe a working AC supply.'))
C('5.2.1-05-M02','In a metal, electron drift compared with conventional current is what?',('Opposite','Electrons carry negative charge.'),('Identical direction','The convention uses positive charge.'),('Perpendicular necessarily','That is not the basic wire relation.'),('Undefined because electrons are neutral','Electrons are negative.'))
C('5.2.2-01','Which quantity is source energy per charge?',('E.m.f.','It describes the source contribution.'),('Power','This is energy per time.'),('Current','This is charge per time.'),('Resistance alone','This is voltage per current.'))
C('5.2.2-04','What does 1 V equal?',('1 J/C','Voltage is energy per charge.'),('1 C/s','That is 1 A.'),('1 J/s','That is 1 W.'),('1 N/m²','That is 1 Pa.'))
C('5.2.1-03','What current transfers 10 C in 5 s?',('2 A','Divide charge by time.'),('50 A','This multiplies them.'),('0.5 A','This reverses the ratio.'),('10 A','This ignores duration.'))
C('5.2.1-03','A current of 0.4 A flows for 20 s. What charge passes?',('8 C','Use It.'),('50 C','This divides time by current.'),('0.02 C','This divides current by time.'),('0.4 C','This ignores duration.'))
C('5.2.2-03','A component transfers 12 J per 3 C. What is V?',('4 V','Divide energy by charge.'),('36 V','This multiplies energy and charge.'),('0.25 V','This reverses the ratio.'),('12 V','This ignores the amount of charge.'))
C('5.2.2-01','Three ideal 1.2 V cells are connected series-aiding. What is their open-circuit voltage?',('3.6 V','Their energy-per-charge contributions add.'),('1.2 V','That ignores series addition.'),('0.4 V','This divides by the number.'),('0 V','They are not opposing one another.'))
C('5.2.1-05-M01','A pulsed current varies but never reverses. How is it classified?',('DC','One-direction flow is the defining feature.'),('AC solely because it varies','Reversal is required for AC.'),('Static charge with no flow','Current is flowing.'),('No electrical quantity','It is a measurable current.'))
